      "claim_text": "SQLite allocates space for every host parameter number up to the largest one used.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 0 states SQLite allocates space to hold all host parameters between 1 and the largest host parameter number used.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "SQLite allocates space to hold all host parameters between 1 and the largest host parameter number used."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "A parameter like ?1000000000 would need gigabytes of memory.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 0 says such a statement would require gigabytes of storage.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "an SQL statement that contains a host parameter like ?1000000000 would require gigabytes of storage."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "SQLITE_MAX_VARIABLE_NUMBER defaults to 999 in versions after 3.32.0.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 0 says the default is 999 for versions prior to 3.32.0 and 32766 for versions after 3.32.0.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "quote": "which defaults to 999 for SQLite versions prior to 3.32.0 (2020-05-22) or 32766 for SQLite versions after 3.32.0."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Recursive triggers have been supported since version 3.6.18.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 1 states beginning with version 3.6.18, recursive triggers are supported.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "Beginning with version 3.6.18, recursive triggers are supported"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Recursive triggers are enabled by default.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 1 says recursive triggers have to be explicitly enabled using PRAGMA recursive_triggers.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 1,
          "quote": "recursive triggers are supported but have to be explicitly enabled using the PRAGMA recursive_triggers statement."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Function arguments are hard-capped at 32767 because the count is sometimes stored in a signed 16-bit integer.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 6 states the number of arguments is sometimes stored in a signed 16-bit integer, giving a hard upper bound on SQLITE_MAX_FUNCTION_ARG of 32767.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 6,
          "quote": "The number of arguments to a function is sometimes stored in a signed 16-bit integer. So there is a hard upper bound on SQLITE_MAX_FUNCTION_ARG of 32767."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "When a write would grow the database past SQLITE_MAX_PAGE_COUNT, SQLite returns SQLITE_FULL.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 2 states an attempt to insert new data that would cause the database file to grow larger than this will return SQLITE_FULL.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 2,
          "quote": "An attempt to insert new data that would cause the database file to grow larger than this will return SQLITE_FULL."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "A table can theoretically hold 18446744073709551616 rows.",
      "verdict": "SUPPORTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 4 states the theoretical maximum number of rows in a table is 264 (18446744073709551616).",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "The theoretical maximum number of rows in a table is 264 (18446744073709551616 or about 1.8e+19)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "A 281 terabyte database is large enough to actually reach that limit.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 4 says this limit is unreachable since the maximum database size of 281 terabytes will be reached first, and a 281 terabyte database can hold no more than approximately 2e+13 rows.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 4,
          "quote": "This limit is unreachable since the maximum database size of 281 terabytes will be reached first. A 281 terabytes database can hold no more than approximately 2e+13 rows"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "claim_text": "Since SQLite 3.45.0 the number of schema objects can be capped with SQLITE_LIMIT_SCHEMA, with a default maximum of 10 million.",
      "verdict": "CONTRADICTED",
      "reasoning": "Passage 5 says the capping with SQLITE_LIMIT_SCHEMA begins in SQLite version 3.54.0, not 3.45.0; the default maximum of 10 million is correct.",
      "citations": [
        {
          "index": 5,
          "quote": "Beginning in SQLite version 3.54.0 (2026-10-09) the number of objects in the schema can be capped using the SQLITE_LIMIT_SCHEMA value to sqlite3_limit(). The default maximum is 10 million"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
